### 1.  Definition of the set \(S_n\)

> “Let  
> \[
> S_n=\{(a,b)\in\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\mid a+b\le n+1\},\qquad |S_n|=\frac{n(n+1)}2 .
> \]”

**Verification:**  
The description of the required points is accurate, and the count \(|S_n|=\frac{n(n+1)}2\) follows from the well‑known formula for the number of integer lattice points in a right‑angled triangle of side‑length \(n\). No issue.

---

### 2.  Reduction to vertical (non‑sunny) lines

> “Assume we have a configuration of \(n\) distinct lines with exactly \(k\) sunny lines.  Replace each non‑sunny line by a vertical line that passes through the leftmost column not yet occupied by a vertical line.  A vertical line covers **all** points of a column, whereas a horizontal line or a line of slope \(-1\) can meet a column in at most one point.  Hence after the replacement we still cover every point of \(S_n\) and we have exactly the same number \(k\) of sunny lines.  
> Consequently we may (and shall) assume that the \(n-k\) non‑sunny lines are the vertical lines  
> \[
> x=1,\;x=2,\dots ,x=n-k .
> \tag{1.1}
> \]”

**Verification:**  
- *Coverage:* A vertical line \(x=c\) indeed contains every point \((c,b)\) with \(b>0\).  Since a horizontal line or a line of slope \(-1\) can intersect a given column in at most one integer point, replacing it by a vertical line cannot lose any covered point.
- *Distinctness:* By choosing the “leftmost column not yet occupied”, the new vertical lines are placed at distinct integer abscissae \(1,2,\dots ,n-k\); thus they remain distinct.
- *Effect on sunny lines:* The sunny lines are left unchanged, so the number of sunny lines stays \(k\).

**Gap:** The solution does not explicitly argue why the replacement does not create a vertical line that coincides with an already existing vertical line (i.e., why the “leftmost column not yet occupied” always exists). This is intuitively clear because there are exactly \(n-k\) non‑sunny lines to be replaced and exactly \(n-k\) columns \(1,\dots ,n-k\) available, but a formal justification is omitted. Hence a **Justification Gap**.

---

### 3.  Description of the remaining points \(T_k\)

> “All points with abscissa \(a\le n-k\) are already covered.  The remaining points are  
> \[
> T_k=\{(x,y)\in\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\mid x+y\le k+1\},
> \tag{1.2}
> \]”

**Verification:**  
If we shift the coordinates by subtracting \(n-k\) from the \(x\)-coordinate, the condition \(a+b\le n+1\) becomes \((x+(n-k))+y\le n+1\), i.e. \(x+y\le k+1\).  Hence the uncovered points form exactly the triangle \(T_k\).  No issue.

---

### 4.  Integer representation of a sunny line

> “Multiplying its equation by a common denominator we obtain an integer equation  
> \[
> p x-q y=c ,\qquad \gcd(p,q)=1,\;q>0,\;p\neq0,\;p\neq -q .
> \tag{2.1}
> \]”

**Verification:**  
Any non‑vertical line can be written as \(y= \frac{p}{q}x - \frac{c}{q}\) with coprime integers \(p,q\) and \(q>0\).  The conditions \(p\neq0\) (non‑horizontal) and \(p\neq -q\) (slope \(\neq-1\)) correctly encode the definition of a sunny line.  No issue.

---

### 5.  Parametrisation of integer points on the line

> “All integer points on the line are described by (3) above:  
> \[
> (x,y)=(x_0+qt,\;y_0+pt),\qquad t\in\mathbb Z .
> \tag{2.2}
> \]”

**Verification:**  
Since \(\gcd(p,q)=1\), the set of integer solutions of \(p x-q y=c\) is indeed a one‑parameter lattice \((x_0+qt,\,y_0+pt)\).  No issue.

---

### 6.  Behaviour of the sum \(x+y\) along the line

> “Their sums satisfy (4):  
> \[
> x+y=(x_0+y_0)+(p+q)t .
> \tag{2.3}
> \]  
> Because the line is sunny, \(p+q\neq0\); therefore the sum changes by at least \(|p+q|\ge1\) when \(t\) varies by one.”

**Verification:**  
The formula follows directly from (2.2).  The condition \(p+q\neq0\) is exactly the exclusion of slope \(-1\).  Hence the sum changes by \(|p+q|\ge1\).  No issue.

