ints on the line are described by (3) above:  \n> \\[\n> (x,y)=(x_0+qt,\\;y_0+pt),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n> \\tag{2.2}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \nSince \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), the set of integer solutions of \\(p x-q y=c\\) is indeed a one\u2011parameter lattice \\((x_0+qt,\\,y_0+pt)\\).  No issue.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Behaviour of the sum \\(x+y\\) along the line\n\n> \u201cTheir sums satisfy (4):  \n> \\[\n> x+y=(x_0+y_0)+(p+q)t .\n> \\tag{2.3}\n> \\]  \n> Because the line is sunny, \\(p+q\\neq0\\); therefore the sum changes by at least \\(|p+q|\\ge1\\) when \\(t\\) varies by one.\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \nThe formula follows directly from (2.2).  The condition \\(p+q\\neq0\\) is exactly the exclusion of slope \\(-1\\).  Hence the sum changes by \\(|p+q|\\ge1\\).  No issue.\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Upper bound for the number of points of a sunny line inside \\(T_k\\)\n\n> \u201cInside the triangle \\(T_k\\) we have \\(2\\le x+y\\le k+1\\).  From (2.3) the number of admissible integers \\(t\\) is at most  \n> \\[\n> \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1-2}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\n> \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1=\n> \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n> \\tag{2.4}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \n- The interval for the sum is correct: the smallest possible sum of two positive integers is\u202f2, the largest allowed in \\(T_k\\) is \\(k+1\\).\n- The number of integer values of \\(t\\) that keep the sum in this interval is indeed \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(k+1)-2}{|p+q|}\\bigr\\rfloor+1 = \\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{|p+q|}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\).\n\n**Gap / Minor Error:**  \nThe inequality \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{|p+q|}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) holds **only when \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\)**.  The case \\(|p+q|=1\\) is not covered here, yet the solution proceeds to use the bound \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) for all sunny lines.  The author later treats \\(|p+q|=1\\) separately, but the transition is not made explicit, leaving a small logical gap.\n\n> \u201cIf \\(|p+q|=1\\) then \\(p+q=\\pm1\\).  The equality \\(p+q=1\\) forces\n> \\(q\\ge2\\) (otherwise \\(p=0\\) which would give a horizontal line).  Because\n> \\(x=x_0+qt\\) must stay in the interval \\([1,k]\\), the number of admissible\n> \\(t\\) is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor k/q\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\bigl\\lfloor(k+1)/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\u201d\n\n**Verification & Gap:**  \n- The condition \\(q\\ge2\\) is correct.\n- The correct bound on the number of admissible \\(t\\) coming from the **\\(x\\)-coordinate** restriction is \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{q}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) (since \\(x\\) must stay between\u202f1 and\u202f\\(k\\)).  The solution writes \\(\\bigl\\lfloor k/q\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\); this is a harmless off\u2011by\u2011one slip that does not affect the final inequality because \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{q}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{q}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  Nevertheless, the statement is imprecise, constituting a **Justification Gap**.\n\n- The inequality \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{q}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) holds for all admissible \\(q\\ge2\\) when \\(k\\) is odd (the case we will need later).  The author does not spell out the elementary number\u2011theoretic verification, so the step is a presentation issue.\n\n> \u201cHence (2.4) holds for **every** sunny line, and the bound is sharp (see \u00a74).\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \nGiven the discussion above, the bound \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) indeed holds for all sunny lines, though the justification required the extra case analysis that was only sketched.  The claim of sharpness is postponed to \u00a74 and is later justified.\n\n> \u201c\\[\n> \\boxed{|\\,\\text{sunny line}\\cap T_k\\,|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor } .\n> \\tag{2.5}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  The boxed inequality follows from the previous discussion, assuming the gaps are filled.  No further issue.\n\n---\n\n### 8.  Counting argument for the whole configuration\n\n> \u201cThe triangle \\(T_k\\) contains  \n> \\[\n> |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n> \\tag{3.1}\n> \\]  \n> If we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, (2.5) yields  \n> \\[\n> k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n> \\tag{3.2}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \nMultiplying the per\u2011line bound by the number of sunny lines gives a necessary condition for covering all points.  The inequality is correct.\n\n> \u201c*Even \\(k\\).*  Write \\(k=2m\\;(m\\ge1)\\).  Then the left\u2011hand side of (3.2) equals\n> \\(2m\\cdot m=m(2m)=k^{2}/2\\), whereas the right\u2011hand side equals\n> \\(m(2m+1)=k^{2}/2+m\\).  Because \\(m>0\\), (3.2) is false.  Hence **no even\n> \\(k\\ge2\\) is possible**.\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \nStraightforward algebra; the inequality fails for any even \\(k\\ge2\\).  No issue.\n\n> \u201c*Odd \\(k\\).*  Write \\(k=2m+1\\;(m\\ge0)\\).  Then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor(k+1)/2\\bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\)\n> and (3.2) becomes an equality:\n> \\[\n> (2m+1)(m+1)=\\frac{(2m+1)(2m+2)}2 .\n> \\tag{3.3}\n> \\]  \n> Consequently every sunny line must contain **exactly** \\(m+1\\) points of\n> \\(T_{2m+1}\\), and the \\(k\\) lines must be pairwise disjoint.\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \nWhen \\(k\\) is odd, the bound is tight, so each line must achieve the maximum and the sets of points covered by different sunny lines cannot overlap.  The deduction is correct.\n\n---\n\n### 9.  Identification of sunny lines that attain the bound\n\n> \u201cEquation (2.1) with \\(|p+q|=2\\) gives three possibilities:\n> * \\(p=q=1\\)\u2003(slope \\(1\\));\n> * \\(p=-2,\\;q=1\\)\u2003(slope \\(-2\\));\n> * \\(p=-1,\\;q=2\\)\u2003(slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).\u201d\n\n**Verification & Gap:**  \n- The first pair \\((p,q)=(1,1)\\) indeed satisfies \\(|p+q|=2\\).  \n- The second pair \\((p,q)=(-2,1)\\) gives \\(p+q=-1\\), so \\(|p+q|=1\\), **not**\u202f2.  \n- The third pair \\((p,q)=(-1,2)\\) gives \\(p+q=1\\), again \\(|p+q|=1\\).  \n\nThus the statement \u201cwith \\(|p+q|=2\\) gives three possibilities\u201d is factually incorrect.  The three listed lines are indeed the only sunny lines that will later be shown to attain the bound, but the classification is wrong.  This is a **Justification Gap** (presentation error that does not affect the final answer).\n\n> \u201cFor \\(k=2m+1\\) the corresponding lines are exactly the three lines listed in (10).  A direct substitution shows that each of them contains \\(m+1\\) points of \\(T_{2m+1}\\) and that the three point\u2011sets are disjoint.  No other sunny line can contain \\(m+1\\) points, because any other choice would have \\(|p+q|\\ge3\\) or \\(|p+q|=1\\) with \\(q\\ge2\\), and then (2.4) gives a strictly smaller upper bound.\u201d\n\n**Verification & Gap:**  \n- The three lines indeed contain exactly \\(m+1\\) points (the construction is correct and can be checked by elementary substitution).  \n- The claim that \u201cany other choice would have \\(|p+q|\\ge3\\) or \\(|p+q|=1\\) with \\(q\\ge2\\)\u201d is true, but the subsequent statement \u201cthen (2.4) gives a strictly smaller upper bound\u201d is **not** correct for the subcase \\(|p+q|=1\\) with \\(q=2\\).  For \\(|p+q|=1\\) and \\(q=2\\) the bound from (2.4) is exactly \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\), not strictly smaller.  The solution therefore does not rule out other lines with \\(|p+q|=1,\\;q=2\\) (e.g. \\(p=-3,\\;q=2\\), slope \\(-\\tfrac32\\)).  A complete argument would need to show that among the \\(|p+q|=1,\\;q=2\\) possibilities only \\(p=-1\\) yields the maximal number of points, which the author omits.  Hence this part contains a **Justification Gap**.\n\n---\n\n### 10.  Consequence for the possible values of \\(k\\)\n\n> \u201cThus, to cover \\(T_{2m+1}\\) with \\(2m+1\\) sunny lines we must use **only**\n> the three lines (10).  Hence we need \\(2m+1\\le3\\); i.e. \\(k=1\\) or\n> \\(k=3\\).\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \nGiven the (partially justified) claim that only three distinct sunny lines can each contain the maximal number of points, the inequality \\(2m+1\\le3\\) follows immediately.  Assuming the previous step is correct, the deduction is valid.  No new gap appears here.\n\n---\n\n### 11.  Explicit constructions for the admissible values\n\n#### (a)  \\(k=0\\)\n\n> \u201cThe \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) are all non\u2011sunny and cover\n> every point of \\(S_n\\).\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \nEach point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) has \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); the vertical line \\(x=a\\) contains it.  The lines are distinct and non\u2011sunny (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  No issue.\n\n#### (b)  \\(k=1\\)\n\n> \u201cUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \n> The only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\).  The sunny line  \n> \\[\n> \\ell:\\; y=2x-2n+1\n> \\]  \n> passes through \\((n,1)\\) and is not parallel to any forbidden direction.\n> Together the \\(n\\) lines satisfy the requirements.\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \n- The vertical lines cover all points with \\(x\\le n-1\\).  \n- The point \\((n,1)\\) indeed satisfies \\(y=2n-2n+1=1\\).  \n- The slope \\(2\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); thus \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  \n- No other point of \\(S_n\\) is missed because any point with \\(x=n\\) must have \\(b\\le1\\) (since \\(n+b\\le n+1\\)), so the only such point is \\((n,1)\\).  Construction is correct.\n\n#### (c)  \\(k=3\\)\n\n> \u201cUse the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  \n> The uncovered set is \\(T_3\\).  The three sunny lines  \n> \\[\n> \\begin{aligned}\n> L_{1}&: y=x,\\\\\n> L_{2}&: y=-2x+5,\\\\\n> L_{3}&: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\n> \\end{aligned}\n> \\]  \n> contain respectively the points  \n> \\[\n> \\begin{array}{l}\n> L_{1}:\\;(1,1),(2,2),\\\\[2pt]\n> L_{2}:\\;(1,3),(2,1),\\\\[2pt]\n> L_{3}:\\;(1,2),(3,1),\n> \\end{array}\n> \\]  \n> which are exactly the six points of \